			<section id="why-privacy">
				<h2>Why Use a Private Search Engine?</h2>
				<p>Every search you make on Google is tracked, stored, and used to build a profile about you. This data is used to:</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Target ads</strong> - Your searches influence ads across the web</li>
					<li><strong>Build profiles</strong> - Companies create detailed profiles of your interests, health concerns, financial situation</li>
					<li><strong>Personalize results</strong> - "Filter bubbles" show you what algorithms think you want to see</li>
					<li><strong>Data breaches</strong> - Your search history can be exposed in security incidents</li>
				</ul>
				<p>Private search engines break this cycle by not tracking your searches in the first place.</p>
			</section>

			<section id="comparison">
				<h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
				<div class="table-wrapper">
					<table>
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th>Engine</th>
								<th>Index Source</th>
								<th>Tracking</th>
								<th>AI Features</th>
								<th>Based In</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<td><strong>DuckDuckGo</strong></td>
								<td>Bing + own</td>
								<td class="status-good">None</td>
								<td>Optional</td>
								<td>USA</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td><strong>Startpage</strong></td>
								<td>Google</td>
